+**Solution.** Add a trust-but-verify fence inside the existing wave-dispatch merge step. Sequence is load-bearing — step 1 captures pre-merge state, step 2 performs the merge and reads post-merge parents, step 3 validates:
 
-1. After `git merge --no-ff <task-branches>` creates the stage commit, read the actual parent SHAs (preserving order) from `git log --format='%P' -n 1 HEAD`.
-2. Capture the expected parent set at wave-dispatch resolution time. **Capture procedure:** immediately before invoking `git merge --no-ff <task-branches>`, the wave-dispatch step captures (a) `git rev-parse HEAD` as the integration-base SHA (will become parent[0] of the `--no-ff` merge), and (b) `git rev-parse refs/heads/<task-NN>` for each task name, writing the integration-base SHA and the task-tip SHA list as separable fields to a runtime sidecar (path Structure's call — described here as "a runtime sidecar under the artifact-dir's review-state tree"). The sidecar is runtime-only — resolved SHAs are NOT written back to `parallelization.md`, preserving the symbolic-only branch-map invariant established in research Q11/Q12. The validation in step 3 reads both fields from this sidecar.
-3. Validate two invariants: (a) `actual_parents[0] == captured_integration_base_sha` (first-parent ordering is load-bearing for the integration spine), and (b) `set(actual_parents[1:]) == set(captured_task_tip_shas)` (full task-tip set match). On either-invariant failure, halt the wave with a named diagnostic: `"stage-commit-parent-mismatch: stage commit <SHA> labeled merge(<task-list>) has actual parents {<actual-set>}, expected {<expected-set>}; first-parent expected <integration-base-sha>, actual <actual-parent-0>; task tips missing: <missing>; unexpected parents present: <extra>"`. Do not advance, do not record the wave as complete, do not let the orchestrator continue.
+1. **Capture expected parents (pre-merge).** Immediately before invoking `git merge --no-ff <task-branches>`, the wave-dispatch step captures (a) `git rev-parse HEAD` as the integration-base SHA (will become parent[0] of the `--no-ff` merge), and (b) `git rev-parse refs/heads/<task-NN>` for each task name, writing the integration-base SHA and the task-tip SHA list as separable fields to a runtime sidecar (path Structure's call — described here as "a runtime sidecar under the artifact-dir's review-state tree"). The sidecar is runtime-only — resolved SHAs are NOT written back to `parallelization.md`, preserving the symbolic-only branch-map invariant established in research Q11/Q12.
+2. **Merge and read actual parents (post-merge).** Run `git merge --no-ff <task-branches>` to create the stage commit, then read the actual parent SHAs (preserving order) from `git log --format='%P' -n 1 HEAD`.
+3. **Validate two invariants.** Read both fields from the sidecar captured in step 1, then check: (a) `actual_parents[0] == captured_integration_base_sha` (first-parent ordering is load-bearing for the integration spine), and (b) `set(actual_parents[1:]) == set(captured_task_tip_shas)` (full task-tip set match). On either-invariant failure, halt the wave with a named diagnostic: `"stage-commit-parent-mismatch: stage commit <SHA> labeled merge(<task-list>) has actual parents {<actual-set>}, expected {<expected-set>}; first-parent expected <integration-base-sha>, actual <actual-parent-0>; task tips missing: <missing>; unexpected parents present: <extra>"`. Do not advance, do not record the wave as complete, do not let the orchestrator continue.
 
 The validation is a wrapper around an existing seam, not new architecture. It runs immediately after the merge so the diagnostic fires at the exact moment the bad commit lands — before any downstream review consumes it.
 
